          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The Jungle Book\" and Children's Literature</strong><br>\nThe Jungle Book, published in 1894, is one of Kipling's most famous works. The collection of stories featuring animals such as Mowgli, Baloo, and Shere Khan teaching life lessons to readers both young and old showcases Kipling's imaginative storytelling and his love for India.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"If—\" and Poetry</strong><br>\nKipling also made a significant contribution to poetry with works like \"If—,\" written in 1895. This poem became one of his best-known works, encapsulating the virtues of stoicism and personal resilience. It remains a classic, often memorized and recited by generations of readers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views and Controversies</strong><br>\nKipling's views on imperialism and race have sparked controversy. His writing reflects a perspective of British superiority and an endorsement of colonialism. Despite this, his storytelling and literary skill continue to be appreciated, while his ideological stance is often critiqued and debated.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
