          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1907, Kipling received the Nobel Prize in Literature, becoming the first English-language writer to receive the award. This accolade recognized his narrative art and mastery in the use of language. His Nobel citation noted his ability to create, with \"aesthetic vigour,\" various moods, scenes, and types.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I and Personal Tragedy</strong><br>\nWorld War I brought personal tragedy for Kipling. His son John joined the Irish Guards and, despite Kipling's efforts to find him after he was reported missing, John was never found. Kipling wrote a history of the Irish Guards, his son's regiment, which was published in 1923, to honor John's memory.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nRudyard Kipling died on January 18, 1936, in London. His legacy as a storyteller remains strong, with works like \"Kim\" and \"The Man Who Would Be King\" adding to his reputation as a master of short stories and novels. Kipling's influence extends beyond literature into the cultural understanding of British imperialism and India's history.</p>\n"
              },
